WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.200
You know, usually when we talk about knowing what's going to happen next, there's this underlying expectation of perfect continuity.

00:07.580 --> 00:10.120
Right, yeah. Like, things should just make sense.

00:10.280 --> 00:16.360
Exactly. Like, think about watching a movie. You're sitting in the theater. You see the villain plant a bomb under a bridge.

00:16.700 --> 00:20.140
You see the hero's train barreling down the tracks toward that exact bridge.

00:20.600 --> 00:21.480
And you know what's coming.

00:21.820 --> 00:25.720
Right. You've just logically pointed the screen and said, okay, that train is going to explode.

00:25.720 --> 00:30.940
Because it's a linear progression. You know, it's cause and effect playing out right in front of your eyes in real time.

00:31.440 --> 00:34.020
The mechanics of the situation are entirely transparent.

00:34.660 --> 00:38.760
They're clean. You have all the pieces on the board, and you can see how they fit together.

00:39.180 --> 00:43.280
But let's be honest. How often does real life actually give you all the pieces?

00:43.580 --> 00:47.220
Oh, almost never. I mean, we wish it did, but it doesn't.

00:47.400 --> 00:48.140
It really doesn't.

00:48.380 --> 00:55.640
Think about how often you, and I mean you listening to this right now, have to make decisions based on incredibly partial information.

00:55.960 --> 00:56.680
Yeah, constantly.

00:57.040 --> 00:59.260
You get an email that's, like, cut off halfway through.

00:59.760 --> 01:03.780
Or you catch a fleeting, totally unreadable expression on your boss's face.

01:04.060 --> 01:07.960
Or you look at a stock chart that only shows the last 24 hours of an asset's price.

01:08.080 --> 01:11.380
Exactly. And suddenly you were expected to predict the future.

01:11.600 --> 01:15.060
It is the absolute definition of predictive muddy waters.

01:15.660 --> 01:22.720
We desperately want reality to be visible and perfectly categorized, you know, laid out in a way that our minds can effortlessly track.

01:22.720 --> 01:26.560
But the universe doesn't operate for our cognitive convenience.

01:26.940 --> 01:28.060
No, it really doesn't.

01:28.800 --> 01:33.900
And when you step into the world of fundamental physics, suddenly that movie projector is completely broken.

01:34.300 --> 01:40.060
Yeah, imagine walking into that same movie theater halfway through the film, but half the screen is covered in duct tape.

01:40.580 --> 01:42.160
The audio is playing in reverse.

01:42.540 --> 01:46.660
And you are still expected to predict the ending with absolute mathematical certainty.

01:46.660 --> 01:51.140
Right. We're looking at a predictive landscape that is incredibly murky.

01:51.760 --> 01:54.460
But what if that murkiness isn't a glitch?

01:54.880 --> 01:55.480
Well, what do you mean?

01:55.860 --> 02:00.140
Like, what if that lack of information is actually a fundamental law of reality?

02:00.340 --> 02:04.080
Ah, right. That is the pivotal shift in perspective.

02:04.460 --> 02:11.760
Moving from seeing missing information as just an engineering problem to seeing it as a structural law of physics.

02:11.760 --> 02:14.960
And that is the exact question we are tackling in today's Deep Dive.

02:15.420 --> 02:19.880
We are undertaking a massive mind-bending mission today.

02:20.360 --> 02:20.860
We really are.

02:21.080 --> 02:27.740
We've got our hands on a recently surfaced October 2026 manuscript by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

02:28.380 --> 02:35.580
It's an incredibly dense, fascinating piece of work titled Admissible Histories and the Structure of Physical Theory.

02:35.940 --> 02:36.740
It's quite a mouthful.

02:36.740 --> 02:46.960
It is. And essentially, Flyxion has mapped out the mathematics of how the universe keeps track of the future when we, the observers, are practically blind.

02:47.340 --> 02:50.660
It is a truly remarkable piece of work.

02:51.380 --> 02:55.280
What Flyxion has done here is monumental in its scope.

02:55.500 --> 02:59.760
I mean, to give you an idea, this isn't just a paper about one niche area of physics.

02:59.940 --> 03:01.260
Right. It's way broader than that.

03:01.260 --> 03:06.720
Exactly. It spans quantum theory, classical thermodynamics, and even macroscopic cosmology.

03:07.020 --> 03:10.980
But it doesn't treat them as separate, disconnected fields with their own rules.

03:11.160 --> 03:12.500
Which is usually how they're treated.

03:12.880 --> 03:19.040
Yeah. But instead, it treats them all as instances of one single, unifying mathematical problem.

03:19.400 --> 03:29.600
That problem is the gap between what the manuscript calls a physical history, which is absolutely everything that has happened into reality, and its observable representation.

03:29.600 --> 03:35.760
Which is just the tiny, fragmented fraction of reality that we actually managed to measure and record.

03:36.160 --> 03:42.720
Exactly. So our mission for you today is to custom tailor this beast of a mathematical text into a shortcut.

03:43.140 --> 03:51.500
We're going to figure out how the literal laws of physics handle the exact same problem you face when you're trying to guess what your boss meant in that half-written email.

03:51.500 --> 04:00.520
It's a fantastic parallel, because that gap between what actually happened and what we see is where all the magic and, honestly, all the profound confusion in physics happens.

04:00.840 --> 04:02.220
Okay. So let's unpack this.

04:02.780 --> 04:09.180
Because the manuscript starts by forcing us to throw away our standard assumptions about what a physical theory even is.

04:09.240 --> 04:09.880
Right out the window.

04:09.880 --> 04:13.940
Yeah. The author introduces this idea of history structures.

04:14.380 --> 04:20.260
And when I first read that, I pictured an incredibly rigid timeline, like a history textbook.

04:20.980 --> 04:22.460
But that's not quite right, is it?

04:22.860 --> 04:23.360
Not at all.

04:23.700 --> 04:27.700
It's actually a very foundational, almost pre-physical concept.

04:27.940 --> 04:33.540
When we think of a physical theory, we usually picture a physicist at a chalkboard writing down differential equations.

04:34.020 --> 04:35.800
Right, like Newton's laws or whatever.

04:35.800 --> 04:39.960
Yeah, Newton's laws, Schrodinger's equation, things that calculate exact trajectories.

04:40.420 --> 04:46.380
But Flyxion argues that before you even get to the equations, a physical theory is fundamentally just a rulebook.

04:46.740 --> 04:48.500
A rulebook. Okay.

04:48.760 --> 04:54.300
It's a rigorous specification of what is allowed to happen next, given what has happened so far.

04:54.920 --> 05:01.380
So it's less about calculating a specific inevitable trajectory, more about, like, defining a menu of options for the future.

05:01.380 --> 05:05.880
Exactly. Think of a history structure as having three components.

05:06.600 --> 05:10.200
First, you have a set of histories, which are all the possible pasts.

05:10.260 --> 05:11.060
Okay, pasts.

05:11.240 --> 05:12.880
Second, you have a set of future segments.

05:13.760 --> 05:20.840
And third, you have a mathematical map that assigns to each specific history its set of admissible continuations.

05:21.600 --> 05:22.920
Admissible continuations.

05:23.440 --> 05:28.520
So the rulebook tells you exactly which futures are legally allowed to follow that specific past.

05:28.660 --> 05:29.520
You've got it.

05:29.520 --> 05:32.980
Let me make sure I've got this fully visualized.

05:33.440 --> 05:44.860
If the theory is completely deterministic, like classical mechanics, where if I hit a billiard ball at a specific angle with a specific force, it has to go to one exact spot, then there is only one allowed future, right?

05:45.160 --> 05:45.640
Spot on.

05:46.220 --> 05:50.580
In a purely deterministic universe, the admissible continuation is singular.

05:51.140 --> 05:53.780
The menu only has one single item on it.

05:53.940 --> 05:54.220
Okay.

05:54.220 --> 06:01.500
But the genius of framing it this way is that this structure perfectly handles stochastic or probabilistic theories, too.

06:02.300 --> 06:02.900
Oh, right.

06:03.280 --> 06:04.340
Like quantum stuff.

06:04.480 --> 06:05.020
Yeah, exactly.

06:05.360 --> 06:10.860
If you were looking at radioactive decay, the menu of admissible futures has multiple items.

06:11.360 --> 06:14.040
The atom might decay in the next second, or it might not.

06:14.400 --> 06:16.460
Both are admissible continuations.

06:16.460 --> 06:21.320
Okay, so the history is the full, unedited record of reality.

06:22.020 --> 06:27.620
But as we established earlier, you and I and our telescopes and microscopes never have the full record.

06:27.800 --> 06:28.920
We just don't have the capacity.

06:29.320 --> 06:32.780
Which brings us to a concept the text calls observation insufficiency.

06:32.780 --> 06:37.800
What does it mean for an observation to be sufficient or insufficient?

06:38.060 --> 06:41.200
Because I usually just think of an observation as, well, looking at something.

06:41.660 --> 06:41.780
Right.

06:41.980 --> 06:42.960
In everyday life, sure.

06:43.480 --> 06:47.220
But in physics, an observation is fundamentally an act of compression.

06:48.000 --> 06:54.200
It's a mathematical map that takes the massive, complex, full history of a system and squashes it down.

06:54.520 --> 06:55.720
It squashes it.

06:56.240 --> 06:57.460
So it's losing stuff.

06:57.840 --> 06:58.220
Yes.

06:58.700 --> 07:00.600
It intrinsically throws information away.

07:00.600 --> 07:10.760
If you look at a thermometer, you are taking the kinetic energy of trillions of individual bouncing air molecules and compressing all of that history into a single number.

07:11.380 --> 07:12.580
Like 72 degrees.

07:12.820 --> 07:13.180
Exactly.

07:13.700 --> 07:17.100
You, the observer, only retain a coarse description of reality.

07:17.760 --> 07:23.880
Okay, so every single time we measure something, we are essentially throwing a massive amount of reality in the trash.

07:24.100 --> 07:25.760
We have to or we'd be overwhelmed.

07:26.520 --> 07:29.920
Now, the crucial question is, did you throw away too much?

07:30.600 --> 07:31.220
How do you know?

07:31.720 --> 07:43.060
An observation is defined as sufficient if whenever two completely different underlying histories happen to look identical to your instrument, they also have the exact same menu of allowed futures.

07:43.320 --> 07:44.040
Wait, hold on.

07:44.500 --> 07:45.780
Let me try to visualize this.

07:46.320 --> 07:51.000
If I have two completely different paths, let's say history A and history B.

07:51.440 --> 07:51.960
Okay.

07:51.960 --> 08:00.260
They took different paths to get here, but my measuring tool, my thermometer or whatever, says they look exactly the same right now.

08:00.380 --> 08:00.620
Right.

08:01.160 --> 08:07.040
For that tool to be sufficient, those two histories better have the exact same possible futures.

08:07.360 --> 08:08.660
That's precisely it.

08:08.660 --> 08:21.240
And if they don't, if history A leads to a fire and history B leads to ice, but my tool says they are currently identical, then my tool is essentially useless for predicting the future.

08:21.240 --> 08:25.760
And that is the strict mathematical definition of insufficiency.

08:26.560 --> 08:32.700
Insufficiency is the phenomenon where two observationally identical presents lead to different admissible futures.

08:32.860 --> 08:33.300
Man.

08:33.640 --> 08:39.900
So you threw away too much information into your compression, and now you are blind to the mechanics of what's going to happen next.

08:40.200 --> 08:41.700
You're completely flying blind.

08:41.700 --> 08:48.180
Which leads to theorem 3.2 in the manuscript, the concept of the minimal sufficient observation.

08:48.620 --> 08:49.780
I absolutely love this idea.

08:50.160 --> 08:52.160
It's a very elegant theorem.

08:52.960 --> 08:57.160
It feels like finding the perfectly packed suitcase for traveling through time.

08:57.260 --> 09:01.000
You want to carry exactly what you need and not an ounce more.

09:01.300 --> 09:02.820
That's a beautiful way to frame it.

09:02.820 --> 09:12.900
Theorem 3.2 is a rigorous mathematical proof showing that for any given history structure, there is a canonical answer to a very specific question.

09:13.040 --> 09:13.540
What's the question?

09:13.800 --> 09:23.860
What is the absolute coarsest, most brutally compressed version of reality you can retain without losing the ability to perfectly predict the future continuations?

09:24.140 --> 09:30.400
So you want to forget as much of the past as humanly possible, but not one single bit more than that limit.

09:30.720 --> 09:31.120
Exactly.

09:31.120 --> 09:33.420
Let's make this concrete for the listener.

09:33.960 --> 09:38.940
The manuscript uses example 1.2, which is the free particle with hidden velocity.

09:39.400 --> 09:39.600
Oh yeah.

09:39.660 --> 09:44.720
Can you walk us through how this minimal sufficient observation works in a real physical scenario?

09:45.260 --> 09:45.780
Gladly.

09:46.480 --> 09:50.500
Imagine a particle moving along a straight line in a vacuum at a constant speed.

09:50.880 --> 09:52.640
Nothing is pushing it or pulling it.

09:53.060 --> 09:53.700
Just coasting.

09:54.000 --> 09:54.860
Yeah, just coasting.

09:54.860 --> 09:59.900
Your history is a long list of its positions recorded at every single second.

09:59.900 --> 10:04.120
Position X0, position X1, position X2, and so on.

10:04.200 --> 10:08.020
All the way up to the present moment, which we'll call position Xn.

10:08.440 --> 10:08.700
Okay.

10:09.320 --> 10:12.880
I have a massive spreadsheet of where this particle has been for the last hour.

10:13.300 --> 10:13.520
Right.

10:13.520 --> 10:21.780
Now, the future you want to predict, the admissible continuation, is the sequence of its displacements from its current position.

10:22.100 --> 10:22.280
Right.

10:22.640 --> 10:23.000
Displacements.

10:23.540 --> 10:23.820
Correct.

10:24.020 --> 10:24.300
Positions.

10:24.560 --> 10:26.380
You don't care where it is on the cosmic grid.

10:26.640 --> 10:31.620
You just want to know how far it's going to move in the next second, the second after that, and so on.

10:31.620 --> 10:37.180
So, my goal is to predict, will it move two inches to the right in the next second, or five inches to the left?

10:37.500 --> 10:37.920
Exactly.

10:38.680 --> 10:42.940
Now, suppose your observation instrument is basically just a camera that takes a snapshot.

10:43.260 --> 10:43.540
Okay.

10:43.680 --> 10:47.160
It only records the present absolute position, Xn.

10:47.420 --> 10:50.740
It throws away the entire spreadsheet of the past, and just tells you,

10:50.740 --> 10:53.420
the particle is currently at the 10-inch mark.

10:53.960 --> 10:57.980
Is that snapshot a sufficient observation to predict the future displacements?

10:58.460 --> 10:58.780
No.

10:59.180 --> 11:03.140
Obviously not, because if I just have a snapshot of a car at a stoplight,

11:03.400 --> 11:05.960
I have absolutely no idea if it's parked with the engine off,

11:06.080 --> 11:08.780
or if it's blowing through the intersection at 100 miles an hour.

11:09.120 --> 11:09.600
Precisely.

11:10.060 --> 11:12.260
Two particles could have completely different histories,

11:12.660 --> 11:16.020
arrive at the exact same position, Xn, at this exact moment,

11:16.200 --> 11:18.240
but have completely different velocities.

11:18.540 --> 11:20.180
One is going fast, one is going slow.

11:20.180 --> 11:23.180
Yeah, and therefore they have completely different futures.

11:23.600 --> 11:29.260
So, recording only the absolute position is fundamentally an insufficient observation.

11:29.740 --> 11:31.200
Okay, so the snapshot fails.

11:31.840 --> 11:34.100
What is the minimal sufficient observation here?

11:34.360 --> 11:35.440
What's the perfectly packed suitcase?

11:35.880 --> 11:36.720
It's the velocity.

11:37.280 --> 11:38.080
Just the velocity.

11:38.340 --> 11:39.260
Just the velocity.

11:40.040 --> 11:42.820
To perfectly predict the future displacements of that particle,

11:43.000 --> 11:46.340
you do not need the giant spreadsheet of everywhere it has ever been.

11:46.420 --> 11:47.560
You can throw all of that away.

11:47.720 --> 11:47.880
Wow.

11:47.880 --> 11:51.600
You actually don't even need its current absolute position.

11:51.800 --> 11:52.880
You can throw that away, too.

11:53.380 --> 11:58.080
You only need to retain the mathematical difference between the last two positions,

11:58.160 --> 11:59.520
which gives you the velocity.

11:59.520 --> 12:05.480
So, that single number, say, five inches per second, is the minimal sufficient observation.

12:06.060 --> 12:11.200
It is the maximum amount of reality you can forget while still predicting the future flawlessly.

12:11.600 --> 12:13.780
You know, this reminds me of reading a mystery novel.

12:14.580 --> 12:18.240
Imagine you, the listener, are trying to figure out who the killer is.

12:18.320 --> 12:18.580
Okay.

12:18.760 --> 12:19.360
I like this.

12:19.720 --> 12:21.860
That's the future continuation you want to predict.

12:21.860 --> 12:28.140
But you decide to be lazy and only read the very last chapter, representing the present state of the book.

12:28.580 --> 12:29.080
The snapshot.

12:29.500 --> 12:29.680
Right.

12:29.920 --> 12:32.900
You open it up, and the detective is pointing a gun at the butler.

12:33.600 --> 12:34.680
But you don't know why.

12:35.260 --> 12:39.040
You don't know if the butler is actually the killer, or if the butler was being framed,

12:39.160 --> 12:41.220
and the detective is making a tragic mistake.

12:41.460 --> 12:42.840
Because you threw away the history.

12:43.260 --> 12:43.680
Exactly.

12:43.680 --> 12:48.620
You have the present state, but it is completely insufficient to predict the resolution.

12:49.360 --> 12:52.940
You need the minimal sufficient observation, which isn't the whole book,

12:53.380 --> 12:58.680
but maybe just a crucial clue hidden in chapter three, and a motive revealed in chapter seven.

12:59.420 --> 13:04.800
That's a fantastic analogy, because it naturally brings up a crucial and mind-bending distinction

13:04.800 --> 13:06.360
that Flyxion makes in the manuscript.

13:06.600 --> 13:06.740
Oh.

13:07.160 --> 13:07.500
What's that?

13:07.740 --> 13:11.500
Well, you just mentioned needing to flip back to chapter three and chapter seven.

13:11.500 --> 13:15.720
This price is a really important question about the nature of memory in physics.

13:16.340 --> 13:21.220
Is there a difference between how much information you need to remember, the size of the state,

13:21.420 --> 13:24.500
and how far back in time you have to look to find it?

13:24.680 --> 13:24.940
Yes.

13:25.320 --> 13:27.020
Wait, so is there a difference?

13:27.320 --> 13:31.740
Because my gut tells me that if I have to look all the way back to chapter one to understand what's

13:31.740 --> 13:34.820
happening now, my required memory must be huge.

13:34.900 --> 13:36.740
Like, I have to keep the whole book in my head.

13:37.140 --> 13:40.960
This is where the manuscript starts to really blow the doors off our everyday intuition.

13:40.960 --> 13:42.020
Okay, I'm ready.

13:42.520 --> 13:47.560
The author explicitly separates a concept called memory depth from the minimal state,

13:47.840 --> 13:52.980
and to mathematically prove that they are entirely different beasts, they introduce

13:52.980 --> 13:57.440
example 18.1, the even process hidden Markov model.

13:57.820 --> 14:03.400
Okay, let's unpack this even process, because this is where the math really started messing

14:03.400 --> 14:04.500
with my head when I read it.

14:04.500 --> 14:05.520
It's a trip.

14:06.060 --> 14:10.380
Imagine you are sitting in a room, and there's a machine on the table spitting out a sequence

14:10.380 --> 14:12.460
of ones and zeros onto a ticker tape.

14:12.920 --> 14:15.320
Okay, so I'm staring at a ticker tape of binary code.

14:15.560 --> 14:15.720
Right.

14:16.560 --> 14:20.640
Internally, inside the black box of the machine, it has two hidden states.

14:21.100 --> 14:22.840
Let's call them state A and state B.

14:23.320 --> 14:26.920
You cannot see A or B. You can only see the ones and zeros it prints.

14:27.180 --> 14:27.780
Just the output.

14:28.060 --> 14:28.560
Just the output.

14:28.560 --> 14:30.920
Now, the machine follows very strict rules.

14:31.440 --> 14:33.860
If it is currently in state A, it has two choices.

14:34.280 --> 14:39.840
It can output a zero and stay in state A, or it can output a one and transition to state

14:39.840 --> 14:40.080
B.

14:40.480 --> 14:41.560
Okay, simple enough.

14:41.920 --> 14:45.220
But if it is in state B, it has absolutely no choice.

14:45.400 --> 14:49.840
It must output a one, and it must immediately transition back to state A.

14:50.400 --> 14:51.380
Let me trace that logic.

14:51.380 --> 14:54.740
So if it's in state B, its hands are tied.

14:55.100 --> 15:00.540
It spits out a one and goes back to A, which means it is physically impossible to ever have

15:00.540 --> 15:05.080
a sequence where you are in B, output a zero, and stay in B.

15:05.540 --> 15:05.800
Correct.

15:06.200 --> 15:09.880
The transition from B to B with a zero simply doesn't exist on the menu.

15:10.480 --> 15:14.720
If you map out the logic of those rules, what this machine is essentially doing is counting

15:14.720 --> 15:17.640
the parity of the unbroken runs of ones.

15:18.120 --> 15:18.480
Parity.

15:18.920 --> 15:19.800
Like even or odd.

15:19.940 --> 15:20.260
Exactly.

15:20.600 --> 15:20.760
Right.

15:20.760 --> 15:25.960
The internal state you are currently in depends entirely on whether the number of ones since

15:25.960 --> 15:28.780
the last zero is an even number or an odd number.

15:29.180 --> 15:33.080
If you've seen an even number of ones in a row, the machine is in state A.

15:33.240 --> 15:36.660
If you've seen an odd number of ones, it is in state B.

15:37.220 --> 15:42.100
Okay, so if I want to predict what the machine might do next, my minimal sufficient observation,

15:42.500 --> 15:43.960
I just need to know the parity.

15:44.420 --> 15:47.180
Is the current run of ones even, or is it odd?

15:48.560 --> 15:49.040
That's it.

15:49.100 --> 15:49.900
That's just two values.

15:49.900 --> 15:51.620
It's a tiny, tiny state.

15:52.060 --> 15:54.720
It's literally one bit of information.

15:54.980 --> 15:55.200
Okay.

15:55.740 --> 15:57.060
So the state is small.

15:57.460 --> 15:58.180
Extremely small.

15:58.300 --> 15:58.480
Okay.

15:58.680 --> 16:02.740
The minimal state required to predict the future has exactly two values.

16:03.100 --> 16:05.180
It fits in the smallest suitcase imaginable.

16:05.180 --> 16:08.680
But now, let's ask about the memory depth.

16:08.900 --> 16:09.300
Oh boy.

16:09.800 --> 16:15.880
How far back along that printed ticker tape do you have to look to definitively figure out

16:15.880 --> 16:18.120
if you are currently in state A or state B?

16:18.340 --> 16:21.200
Well, I just have to trace my finger back to the last zero, right?

16:21.700 --> 16:23.380
Because the zero acts as a reset button.

16:23.920 --> 16:28.800
Once I see a zero, I know for a fact the machine went to state A, and then I just count the ones

16:28.800 --> 16:30.300
after it to see if it's even or odd.

16:30.300 --> 16:31.880
That is absolutely correct.

16:32.140 --> 16:32.360
But?

16:32.980 --> 16:34.400
What if you haven't seen a zero yet?

16:34.740 --> 16:37.620
What if you look back 10 steps on the tape, and it's all ones?

16:37.960 --> 16:39.520
Then I have to look back 11 steps.

16:39.800 --> 16:41.980
And what if it's all ones for 100 steps?

16:42.220 --> 16:44.820
Then I have to keep pulling the tape and look back 101 steps.

16:44.820 --> 16:48.000
Do you see the trap the universe just set for you?

16:48.200 --> 16:48.600
Oh, wow.

16:49.140 --> 16:54.860
In principle, you might have to look back infinitely far into the past to find that last zero.

16:55.600 --> 17:00.420
So the memory depth, the size of the temporal window you need to guarantee you can determine

17:00.420 --> 17:02.140
the state, is infinite.

17:02.900 --> 17:03.260
Whoa.

17:03.820 --> 17:04.220
Hold on.

17:04.460 --> 17:05.560
That feels like a contradiction.

17:05.800 --> 17:11.340
So the minimal state is incredibly tiny, just one single bit, even or odd.

17:11.340 --> 17:16.240
